hvf: give the guest a data abort for an MMIO access QEMU can't emulate

A data abort without a valid ISV (a load or store pair, SIMD or SVE, or a
write-back access) to an address that isn't RAM asserted, and QEMU ended.
Windows' 3D driver makes such an access to the GPU's memory window, in the
PCI MMIO window, as Windows shuts down, so a machine with graphics
acceleration ended QEMU on a graceful stop. The guest now gets the
synchronous external abort that an access to a missing device gives.

--- a/target/arm/hvf/hvf.c	2026-11-07 23:19:27
+++ b/target/arm/hvf/hvf.c	2026-11-07 23:19:27
@@ -2457,12 +2457,26 @@
          */
         assert(!s1ptw);
 
-        /*
-         * TODO: ISV will be 0 for SIMD or SVE accesses.
-         * Inject the exception into the guest.
-         */
-        assert(isv);
+        if (!isv) {
+            /*
+             * A pair, SIMD or SVE access, or one that writes its address
+             * back, gives no register or size to emulate it with. The guest
+             * gets the abort a device that isn't there gives, rather than
+             * QEMU ending: Windows' 3D driver makes such an access to the
+             * GPU's memory window as Windows shuts down, and the process
+             * that made it ends instead of the machine.
+             */
+            int target_el = 1;
 
+            cpu_synchronize_state(cpu);
+            env->exception.vaddress = excp->virtual_address;
+            hvf_raise_exception(cpu, EXCP_DATA_ABORT,
+                                syn_data_abort_no_iss(arm_current_el(env) == target_el,
+                                                      0, 0, 0, 0, iswrite, 0x10),
+                                target_el);
+            break;
+        }
+
         /*
          * Emulate MMIO.
          * TODO: Inject faults for errors.
